## Hotfix · slice-4.1 (live verification revealed two schema/LLM mismatches)

```
finding A   accountant email: Summary.amounts schema rejects None elements
            LLM returns list[None] for docs with no monetary mentions

finding B   lumina contract: ContractReview.parties/payment_terms schema rejects dicts
            LLM returns rich {name, role} / {description, ...} objects · schema declared list[str]
            this is INFORMATION LOSS being prevented by the schema — fix by relaxing types,
            not by forcing the LLM down to strings

finding C   eval commit "0970fbb fix ContractReview+Summary coercers · relax fixture
            language checks" should be audited — relaxing fixture checks defeats the harness
            purpose; if checks were trimmed to make them pass, restore them

scope IN    schema relaxation · reseed · verify · audit eval fixture relaxation
scope OUT   any new analyzers · UI changes · feature work
```

### Hotfix builder prompt

Copy into a fresh Sonnet session at the repo root:

```text
Read CLAUDE.md and ops/slice-4.classifier-summary-pdf-eval.hybrid §Hotfix.

You are the builder for slice-4 hotfix. Coordinator (opus) verified the live pipeline; production data exposed two schema gaps that the analyzer pattern caught honestly (audit events written, no silent failures), but extraction is incomplete.

Three findings, three fixes:

A. apps/api/app/schemas/summary.py — Summary.amounts (and any analogous list[str] fields like deadlines, obligations, key_points, missing_information) reject None elements. The LLM correctly returns [None] when no values exist.

B. apps/api/app/schemas/contract.py — ContractReview.parties, payment_terms, obligations, termination_terms, penalties (and similar list fields) declared as list[str] but the LLM returns list of dicts ({name, role} for parties; {description, ...} for payment_terms). The dict shape is RICHER and we should preserve it, not coerce down.

C. Audit commit 0970fbb. Inspect apps/api/app/evals/fixtures.py and apps/api/app/evals/runner.py. If language-check relaxations weakened the fixtures (dropped expected language assertions, lowered score thresholds, removed expected fields), restore them. Eval discipline only works if it stays honest.

Tasks (commit per checkpoint):

1. SUMMARY SCHEMA RELAXATION.
   In apps/api/app/schemas/summary.py:
   - For list-of-string fields (amounts, deadlines, obligations, key_points, missing_information): keep type list[str] but add a field_validator(mode="before") that drops None elements before validation.
   - Mental tests: input [None] → []; input ["3000 RON", None, "VAT 19%"] → ["3000 RON", "VAT 19%"]; input None → [].
   Commit: "slice-4-hotfix(summary): drop None elements from list fields"

2. CONTRACTREVIEW SCHEMA RELAXATION.
   In apps/api/app/schemas/contract.py:
   - For parties, payment_terms, obligations, termination_terms, penalties, important_dates, risk_flags, questions_for_human_review: change type from list[str] to list[str | dict[str, Any]]. Accept either shape; preserve LLM structure.
   - Add a field_validator(mode="before") that drops None elements.
   - Do NOT introduce sub-Pydantic-models (Party, PaymentTerm) yet — slice-N when the UI knows what to do with structured data. For slice-4 we just preserve what the LLM returned.
   - Frontend already renders these as JSON in analyzer_outputs.contract_reviewer so flexible shape is fine.
   Commit: "slice-4-hotfix(contract): accept str|dict in list fields · preserve LLM structure"

3. EVAL FIXTURE AUDIT.
   - git show 0970fbb to see what was changed.
   - For each language-check relaxation: was expected language assertion removed (e.g., expected "ro" was dropped)? If yes, restore it.
   - For other relaxations: judgement — if the fixture's expected output was watered down to match a buggy LLM response, restore the original expectation. Fixtures are ground truth; if the LLM doesn't meet them, eval should fail.
   - If a relaxation was correct (e.g., a typo in the original fixture), document the reasoning in patchlog and keep it.
   Commit: "slice-4-hotfix(evals): restore fixture rigor (or document why relaxation was correct)"

4. WIPE + RESEED.
   - cd apps/api && uv run alembic downgrade base && uv run alembic upgrade head
   - uv run python scripts/seed_demo.py (or whatever the slice-4 builder named it)
   - All 7 docs should land status="done".

5. VERIFY.
   - curl http://localhost:8000/api/documents | jq '.[] | {filename, status}' → all 7 "done"
   - Accountant email: analysis.summary populated · analyzer_outputs.summarizer.amounts == [] (not [None]).
   - Contract: analyzer_outputs.contract_reviewer with parties as list of dicts (preserving name/role), payment_terms as list of dicts (preserving description), etc.
   - Run eval harness: uv run python -m app.evals.run
   - Capture: pass count, fail count, which fixtures fail. The PO-ref intentional fail should still fail (it's the regression-catcher); other failures should be zero post-hotfix.

6. SHIP.
   - Append ONE PATCHLOG line to ops/slice-4.classifier-summary-pdf-eval.hybrid (timestamp · model · summary · eval pass-rate).
